# Line endings, decided once here instead of per machine.
#
# Why this file exists (2026-10-05): this machine has `core.autocrlf=true`, so a
# fresh clone checked every text file out as CRLF while the Linux CI checkout is
# LF. `scripts/lan-store.mjs` starts with `#!/usr/bin/env node`, and vitest 1
# feeds the transformed module to `vm.runInThisContext`, which rejects a shebang
# line that ends in `\r` with `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token`. On
# Windows that silently took out `tests/lan/test_lan_store.test.ts` and
# `tests/training/test_data_cli.test.ts` — the only two root suites that import a
# shebang script — while CI stayed green and the failure looked like a mystery.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the working tree byte-identical on every platform: the same bytes
# in the editor, in vitest, in Rust's build and in CI. Only the Windows launchers
# below keep CRLF, because cmd.exe and the double-click path are the least
# forgiving readers in the repo.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows launchers and PowerShell scripts: cmd.exe's `goto`/labels and the
# double-click .bat path are the constructs that misbehave with bare LF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binaries — never touched by the eol machinery.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.wasm binary
*.exe binary
*.msi binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.traineddata binary